"1989-aime-13": { s: String.raw`Let $S$ be a subset of $\{1,2,3,\ldots,1989\}$ such that no two members of $S$ differ by $4$ or $7.$ What is the largest number of elements $S$ can have?`, u: "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/aime/1989" },
"1989-aime-14": { s: String.raw`Given a positive integer $n,$ it can be shown that every complex number of the form $r+si,$ where $r$ and $s$ are integers, can be uniquely expressed in the base $-n+i$ using the integers $0,$ $1,$ $\ldots,$ $n^2$ as digits. That is, the equation$$r+si={} a_m(-n+i)^m +a_{m-1}(-n+i)^{m-1} +\cdots+a_1(-n+i) +a_0$$is true for a unique choice of nonnegative integer $m$ and digits $a_0,$ $a_1,$ $\ldots,$ $a_m$ chosen from the set $\{0,1,2,\ldots,n^2\},$ with $a_m\ne0.$ We write$$r+si=(a_ma_{m-1}\ldots a_1a_0)_{-n+i}$$to denote the base $-n+i$ expansion of $r+si.$ There are only finitely many integers $k+0i$ that have four-digit expansions$$k=(a_3a_2a_1a_0)_{-3+i}\qquad a_3\ne0.$$Find the sum of all such $k.$`, u: "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/aime/1989" },
